[[File:Sydneycityscape.jpg|thumb|upright=1.36|Sydney as viewed from [[Tasman Sea]], overlooking the sandstone cliffs in [[Vaucluse, New South Wales|Vaucluse]]]]
[[File:ISS022-E-75960 - View of New South Wales.jpg|thumb|270px|Satellite photo of the Sydney area]]
The '''geography of Sydney''' is characterised by its coastal location on a basin bordered by the [[Pacific Ocean]] to the east, the [[Blue Mountains (New South Wales)|Blue Mountains]] to the west, the [[Hawkesbury River]] to the north and the [[Woronora Plateau]] to the south. [[Sydney]] lies on a [[submergent coastline]] on the east coast of [[New South Wales]], where the ocean level has risen to flood deep river valleys ([[ria]]s) carved in the [[Sydney sandstone]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1029/2019JG005192 |title=Carbon Budget for a Large Drowned River Valley Estuary Adjacent to an Emerging Megacity (Sydney Harbour)
 |work=American Geophysical Union |publisher=[[Wiley (publisher)|Wiley]] |author = E. L. Tanner and B. D. Eyre|year=2019 |access-date=11 December 2024}}</ref> [[Port Jackson]], better known as Sydney Harbour, is one such ria.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.environment.gov.au/parks/nrs/science/bioregion-framework/ibra/index.html |title=Australia's bioregions (IBRA) |work=Department of Sustainability, Environment, Water, Population and Communities |publisher=Commonwealth of Australia |year=2012 |access-date=13 January 2013}}</ref>

The Sydney area lies on [[Triassic]] [[shales]] and [[sandstones]]. The region mostly consists of low rolling hills and wide valleys in a [[rain shadow]] area that is shielded by the [[Great Dividing Range]].<ref>[https://www.willyweather.com.au/news/5291/rain+shadows.html Rain Shadows] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210922232632/https://www.willyweather.com.au/news/5291/rain+shadows.html |date=22 September 2021 }} by Don White. Australian Weather News. Willy Weather. Retrieved 24 May 2021.</ref> Sydney sprawls over two major regions: the [[Cumberland Plain]], a relatively flat region lying to the west of Sydney Harbour, and the [[Hornsby Plateau]], a [[plateau]] north of the Harbour rising to 200 metres and dissected by steep valleys.<ref>[http://www.environment.nsw.gov.au/bioregions/SydneyBasin-Subregions.htm Sydney Basin-Subregions]</ref> Sydney's native plant species are predominantly [[eucalyptus tree]]s,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.agriculture.gov.au/abares/forestsaustralia/profiles/eucalypt-forest|title=Eucalypt forest|access-date=29 January 2017|publisher=Commonwealth of Australia|year=2017}}</ref> and its [[soils]] are usually red and yellow in texture. The endemic flora is home to a variety of [[Birds of Australia|bird]], [[insect]], [[List of reptiles of Australia|reptile]] and [[Mammals of Australia|mammal]] species, which are conspicuous in urban areas.{{sfn|Herbert|Helby|1980|p=582}}

There are more than 70 harbour and ocean beaches in the urban area. Most of Sydney's water storages are on tributaries of the [[Nepean River]]. [[Parramatta River]] drains a large area of Sydney's western suburbs.<ref>{{cite book |title=The Water Supply, Sewerage and Drainage of Sydney |author=Aird, W. V |year=1961 |publisher=[[Sydney Water|Metropolitan Water, Sewerage and Drainage Board]] |location=Sydney }}</ref> With 5,005,400 inhabitants (as of 2016) and an urban population density of 2037 people per square kilometre, Sydney's [[urban area]] covers {{convert|1788|km2|sqmi|0|abbr=on}},<ref name="2016 Census">{{cite web|url=http://www.abs.gov.au/ausstats/abs%40.nsf/mediareleasesbyCatalogue/28F51C010D29BFC9CA2575A0002126CC?OpenDocument|title=Sydney population hits 5 million|publisher=[[Australian Bureau of Statistics]]|date=30 March 2017|access-date=5 April 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170728200642/http://www.abs.gov.au/ausstats/abs%40.nsf/mediareleasesbyCatalogue/28F51C010D29BFC9CA2575A0002126CC?OpenDocument|archive-date=28 July 2017|url-status=dead}}</ref> comprising 35% of Sydney and is constantly growing.<ref name="abs_2016_0">{{Census 2006 AUS|id=105|name=Sydney (Statistical Division)|accessdate=2008-02-28}}</ref>

{{Short description|Compound with carbon to copper bonds}}
{{Use American English|date=January 2019}}
{{Use mdy dates|date=January 2019}}
[[File:Lithium-diphenylcuprate-dietherate-dimer-from-xtal-3D-sticks-C.png|200px|thumb|Lithium diphenylcuprate etherate dimer from crystal structure]] 
[[File:Lithium-diphenylcuprate-etherate-dimer-from-xtal-2D-skeletal.png|200px|thumb|Skeletal formula of lithium diphenylcuprate etherate dimer]]
'''Organocopper chemistry''' is the study of the physical properties, reactions, and synthesis of '''organocopper compounds''', which are [[organometallic compounds]] containing a [[carbon]] to [[copper]] [[chemical bond]].<ref>{{cite book|title=An introduction to synthesis using organocopper reagents|author=Gary H. Posner|year=1980|isbn=0-471-69538-6|publisher=Wiley|location=New York: Wiley|author-link=Gary H. Posner|url-access=registration|url=https://archive.org/details/introductiontosy00posn}}</ref><ref>{{cite book|title=Synthetic Methods of Organometallic and Inorganic Chemistry|volume= 5, Copper, Silver, Gold, Zinc, Cadmium, and Mercury|editor=W.A. Herrmann|isbn=3-13-103061-5|year=1999|publisher=Thieme|location=Stuttgart}}</ref><ref>{{cite book|title=Organometallics|author=Christoph Elschenbroich|edition=3|year=2006|isbn=3-527-29390-6|publisher=Wiley-VCH|place=Weinheim}}</ref> They are [[reagents]] in [[organic chemistry]].

The first organocopper compound, the explosive [[copper(I) acetylide]] {{chem2|Cu2C2}} ({{chem2|Cu+[-C\tC-]Cu+}}), was synthesized by [[Rudolf Christian Böttger]] in 1859 by passing [[acetylene]] gas through a solution of [[copper(I) chloride]] in ammonia:<ref>{{cite journal|author=R. C. Böttger|journal=Annalen der Chemie und Pharmacie|volume=109|year=1859|pages=351–362|title = Ueber die Einwirkung des Leuchtgases auf verschiedene Salzsolutionen, insbesondere auf eine ammoniakalische Kupferchlorürlösung|doi = 10.1002/jlac.18591090318|issue=3|url=https://zenodo.org/record/1427127}}</ref>
:{{chem2|C2H2 + 2 [Cu(NH3)2]Cl → Cu2C2 + 2 NH4Cl + 2 NH3}}

{{Short description|Astronomical survey}}
{{Infobox astronomical survey}}
[[File:Extended Groth Strip.jpg|thumb|300px|[[Extended Groth Strip]] taken with Hubble’s Advanced Camera for Surveys.<ref name=esa-hubble>{{cite news|title=A Snapshot of Galactic Evolution|url=http://spacetelescope.org/images/potw1141a/|accessdate=11 October 2011|newspaper=ESA/Hubble|date=10 October 2011}}</ref>]]

'''AEGIS''', or the '''All-Wavelength Extended Groth Strip International Survey''', is a multi-wavelength astronomical survey of a patch of the sky with low extinction and zodiacal scattering. The purpose of the survey is to study the physical processes and evolution of [[galaxy|galaxies]] at [[redshift]] z ~ 1.  As of February 2011 more than 80 research papers have been published based on data from the survey.<ref>[http://aegis.ucolick.org/aegis_papers.html AEGIS Papers] retrieved February 10, 2011</ref>

{{Short description|Broad tradition of Western art music}}
{{About|Western art music from the Middle Ages to the present|specific period and associated style known as Classical|Classical period (music)|other uses|Classical music (disambiguation)}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=November 2023}}
[[File:BJO Konzert Phil MR 098.jpg|thumb|upright=1.35|Members of a [[youth orchestra]] standing to acknowledge [[applause]] after performing.]]
'''Classical music''' is a tradition of [[art music]] in the [[Western world]], considered to be [[#Relationship to other music traditions|distinct from]] Western [[folk music]] or [[popular music]]. It is sometimes distinguished as '''Western classical music''', as the term "classical music" can also be applied to [[List of classical and art music traditions|non-Western art musics]]. Classical music is often characterized by formality and complexity in its [[musical form]] and [[Harmony|harmonic organization]],{{sfn|Owens|2008|loc=§ para. 1}} particularly with the use of [[polyphony]].{{sfn|Schulenberg|2000|p=99}} Since at least the ninth century, it has been primarily a written tradition,{{sfn|Schulenberg|2000|p=99}} spawning a sophisticated [[music notation|notational]] system, as well as accompanying literature in [[music analysis|analytical]], [[music criticism|critical]], [[Music history|historiographical]], [[musicology|musicological]] and [[Philosophy of music|philosophical]] practices.

Rooted in the [[patronage]] of [[Christian Church|churches]] and [[royal court]]s in Europe,{{sfn|Owens|2008|loc=§ para. 1}} surviving early [[medieval music]] is chiefly [[sacred music|religious]], [[Monophony|monophonic]] and vocal. The earliest extant music manuscripts date from the [[Carolingian Empire]] (800–887),{{sfn|Schulenberg|2000|p=100}} around the time which Western [[plainchant]] gradually unified into what is termed [[Gregorian chant]].{{sfn|Schulenberg|2000|pp=100–101}} Musical centers existed at the [[Abbey of Saint Gall]], the [[Saint Martial school|Abbey of Saint Martial]] and [[Saint Emmeram's Abbey]], while the 11th century saw the development of [[Staff (music)|staff notation]] and increasing output from [[list of medieval music theorists|medieval music theorists]]. By the mid-12th century, France became the major European musical center:{{sfn|Schulenberg|2000|p=100}} the religious [[Notre-Dame school]] first fully explored [[Rhythmic mode|organized rhythm]]s and [[polyphony]], while [[secular]] music flourished with the [[troubadour]] and ''[[trouvère]]'' traditions led by poet-musician nobles.{{sfn|Schulenberg|2000|pp=102–104}} This culminated in the court-sponsored French ''[[ars nova]]'' and Italian [[Music of the Trecento|Trecento]], which evolved into {{lang|la|[[ars subtilior]]}}, a stylistic movement of extreme rhythmic diversity.{{sfn|Schulenberg|2000|pp=102–104}} Beginning in the early 15th century, [[Renaissance music|Renaissance]] composers of the influential [[Franco-Flemish School]] built on the harmonic principles in the English ''[[contenance angloise]]'', bringing choral music to new standards, particularly the [[Mass (music)|mass]] and [[motet]].{{sfn|Schulenberg|2000|pp=104–105}} Northern Italy soon emerged as the central musical region, where the [[Roman School (music)|Roman School]] engaged in highly sophisticated methods of polyphony in genres such as the [[madrigal]],{{sfn|Schulenberg|2000|pp=104–105}} which inspired the brief [[English Madrigal School]].

The [[Baroque music|Baroque period]] (1580–1750) saw the relative standardization of [[Common practice period|common-practice]] [[tonality]],{{sfn|Schulenberg|2000|p=110}} as well as the increasing importance of [[musical instruments]], which grew into ensembles of considerable size. Italy remained dominant, being the [[Origins of opera|birthplace of opera]], the soloist centered [[concerto]] genre, the organized [[sonata form]] as well as the large scale vocal-centered genres of [[oratorio]] and [[cantata]]. The [[fugue]] technique championed by [[Johann Sebastian Bach]] exemplified the Baroque tendency for complexity, and as a reaction the simpler and song-like [[galant music]] and ''[[Empfindsamkeit (music)|empfindsamkeit]]'' styles were developed. In the shorter but pivotal [[Classical period (music)|Classical period]] (1750–1820), composers such as [[Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart]], [[Joseph Haydn]], and [[Ludwig van Beethoven]] created widely admired representatives of [[absolute music]],{{sfn|Schulenberg|2000|p=113}}{{sfn|Owens|2008|loc=§ para. 2}} including [[symphony|symphonies]], [[string quartet]]s and concertos. The subsequent [[Romantic music]] (1800–1910) focused instead on [[Program music|programmatic music]], for which the [[art song]], [[symphonic poem]] and various [[piano]] genres were important vessels. During this time [[Virtuoso|virtuosity]] was celebrated, immensity was encouraged, while philosophy and [[musical nationalism|nationalism]] were embedded—all aspects that converged in the [[List of operas by Richard Wagner|operas of Richard Wagner]].

By the 20th century, stylistic unification gradually dissipated while the prominence of popular music greatly increased. Many composers actively avoided past techniques and genres in the lens of [[Modernism (music)|modernism]], with some abandoning tonality in place of [[serialism]], while others found new inspiration in folk melodies or [[Impressionism in music|impressionist]] sentiments. After World War II, for the first time audience members valued older music over [[contemporary classical music|contemporary]] works, a preference which has been catered to by the emergence and widespread availability of commercial recordings.{{sfn|Owens|2008|loc=§ para. 7}} Trends of the mid-20th century to the present day include [[New Simplicity]], [[New Complexity]], [[Minimal music|Minimalism]], [[Spectral music]], and more recently [[Postmodern music]] and [[Postminimalism]]. Increasingly global, practitioners from the Americas, Africa and Asia have obtained crucial roles,{{sfn|Schulenberg|2000|p=100}} while symphony orchestras and [[opera house]]s now appear across the world.

