'''Latvian literature''' began to develop in the 18th century. Latvian secular literature began with [[Gotthard Friedrich Stender]] who produced didactic tales or idyllic portrayals of country life.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Latvian literature {{!}} History, Poetry & Prose {{!}} Britannica |url=https://www.britannica.com/art/Latvian-literature |access-date=2024-05-06 |website=www.britannica.com |language=en}}</ref>

{{Short description|Structured book club for young people}}
{{Reading}}{{Redirect|Literature club|other uses|Book club (disambiguation){{!}}book club}}
A '''literature circle''', or '''literature club''', is equivalent for [[young people]] of an adult [[Book discussion club|book club]], but with greater structure, expectation and rigor. The aim is to encourage thoughtful discussion and a love of reading in young people. The intent of literature circles is "to allow students to practice and develop the skills and strategies of good readers" (DaLie, 2001).

