{{DISPLAYTITLE:Etymology of ''chemistry''}}
{{Wiktionary|chemistry}}

The word [[chemistry]] derives from the word [[alchemy]], which is found in various forms in European languages.

The word ''alchemy'' itself derives from the [[Arabic]] word {{translit|ar|al-kīmiyāʾ}} ({{lang|ar|الكيمياء}}), wherein ''[[al-]]'' is the definite article 'the'. The ultimate origin of the word is uncertain,<ref>[[Encyclopædia Britannica]], 2002 Edition, CD-ROM</ref> but the Arabic term ''kīmiyāʾ'' ({{lang|ar|كيمياء}}) is likely derived from either the [[Ancient Greek]] word {{translit|grc|khēmeia}} ({{lang|grc|χημεία}}) or the similar {{translit|grc|khēmia}} ({{lang|grc|χημία}}).<ref name=oed>"alchemy", entry in ''The Oxford English Dictionary'', J. A. Simpson and E. S. C. Weiner, vol. 1, 2nd ed., 1989, {{ISBN|0-19-861213-3}}.</ref><ref>p. 854, "Arabic alchemy", Georges C. Anawati, pp. 853-885 in ''Encyclopedia of the history of Arabic science'', eds. Roshdi Rashed and Régis Morelon, London: Routledge, 1996, vol. 3, {{ISBN|0-415-12412-3}}.</ref>

The Greek term ''khēmeia'', meaning 'cast together',<ref>Weekley, Ernest (1967). Etymological Dictionary of Modern English. New York: Dover Publications. {{ISBN|0-486-21873-2}}</ref> may refer to the art of alloying metals, from root words {{lang|grc|χύμα}} ({{translit|grc|khúma}}, 'fluid') and {{lang|grc|χέω}} ({{translit|grc|khéō}}, 'I pour').<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.etymonline.com/word/alchemy|title=Alchemy &#124; Origin and meaning of alchemy by Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref> Alternatively, ''khēmia'' may be derived from the ancient [[Egyptian language|Egyptian]] name of [[Egypt#Names|Egypt]], {{translit|egy|khem}}, {{translit|egy|khm}}, {{translit|egy|khame}}, or {{translit|egy|khmi}}, meaning 'blackness', likely in reference to the rich dark soil of the [[Nile]] river valley.<ref name=oed />
